\section{Choosing one}{

For a world choropleth the honest choice is \strong{equal-area}, because the eye
reads coloured area as quantity: a projection that inflates Greenland makes
Greenland's value look more important than it is. Equal Earth is the
recommended default (and the package's own) -- it is equal-area, close to
Robinson in appearance, and cheap to evaluate (Savric, Patterson & Jenny
2019). \code{"mercator"} is conformal, not equal-area, and should not be used for
choropleths. Use \code{\link[=projection_compare]{projection_compare()}} to see the difference on your own
data, and \code{\link[=tissot_map]{tissot_map()}} to see it on the graticule.
}

